static char sx__str1032[]=" # Spectre Language Reference\n\n ---\n\n ## Comments\n\n ```\n // This is a line comment\n ```\n\n ---\n\n ## Variables\n\n ```\n val x: i64 = 42          // immutable binding\n val y: mut i64 = 0       // mutable binding\n y = y + 1\n ```\n";
static char sx__str1033[]=" ---\n\n ## Primitive Types\n\n | Type    | Description              |\n |---------|--------------------------|\n | `i32`   | 32-bit signed integer    |\n | `i64`   | 64-bit signed integer    |\n | `u8`    | 8-bit unsigned integer   |\n | `u32`   | 32-bit unsigned integer  |\n | `u64`   | 64-bit unsigned integer  |\n | `usize` | pointer-sized unsigned   |\n | `f32`   | 32-bit float             |\n | `f64`   | 64-bit float             |\n | `bool`  | boolean (`true`/`false`) |\n | `u8`  | single byte character    |\n | `void`  | no value                 |\n";
static char sx__str1034[]=" ---\n\n ## Reference and Pointer Types\n\n ```\n val p: ref u8 = \"hello\"    // pointer to u8 (C string)\n val q: mut ref i64 = ...     // reassignable pointer\n ```\n\n `ref T` is a raw pointer.  Null is written as `none`.\n\n ---\n\n ## List Type\n\n ```\n val ac: allocators.HeapAllocator = @zeroed\n val xs: mut list[i64] = @listalloc[allocators.HeapAllocator, i64](ac)?\n @append(xs, 1)\n @append(xs, 2)\n val n: usize = @len(xs)\n val opt = @get(xs, 0)        // returns option[i64]\n @remove(xs, 0)\n @reserve(xs, 64)\n @listfree(xs)\n ```\n";
static char sx__str1035[]=" ---\n\n ## Option Type\n\n ```\n val a: option[i64] = some 42\n val b: option[i64] = none\n\n match a {\n     some v => { /* use v */ }\n     none   => { /* handle missing */ }\n }\n ```\n\n ---\n\n ## Result Type\n\n ```\n val r: result[i64, ref u8] = ok 10\n val e: result[i64, ref u8] = err \"oops\"\n\n match r {\n     ok v  => { } // Use v\n     err e => { } // Handle the error\n }\n ```\n\n Note that braces in match arms are not optional. You must use braces, for example:\n\n ```\n ok v => hello // INVALID\n ok v => { return hello } // VALID if hello is defined and matches the return type\n ```";
static char sx__str1036[]=" ---\n\n ## Strings\n\n ```\n val s: ref u8 = \"hello\"           // C string literal\n val raw = \\\\ a raw string literal   // raw string (no escape processing)\n           \\\\ continued on next line // lines joined with \\n\n ```\n\n The standard library (`std/string`) provides `String` (owned heap string)\n and `StringBuilder` for building strings dynamically.\n\n ---\n\n ## Functions\n\n ```\n fn add(a: i64, b: i64) i64 = {\n     return a + b\n }\n\n pub fn greet(name: ref u8) void = {\n     @print(\"Hello, %s!\\n\", name)\n }\n\n pub enum SomeError = {\n     FailedToDoSomeThing\n     OrSomethingElse\n }\n\n // Functions that may propagate errors use result[T, E]\n fn open_file(path: ref u8) result[ref void, SomeError] = {\n     // ... can use ? to propagate\n }\n ```\n";
static char sx__str1037[]=" ---\n\n ## Namespaced Functions\n\n ```\n type Counter = {\n     value: mut i64\n }\n\n pub fn (Counter) new() ref Counter = {\n     val c: ref Counter = trust @alloc(@sizeof(Counter)) as ref Counter\n     // Note that \"trust\" needs to be used above as @alloc is an untrusted intrinsic\n     // as per the contract system.\n     c.value = 0\n     return c\n }\n\n pub fn (Counter) inc(s: mut self) void = {\n     s.value = s.value + 1\n }\n\n // Call site:\n val c = Counter.new()\n Counter.inc(c)\n ```\n\n `self` in a parameter position refers to the enclosing type.\n `mut self` means the method may mutate the receiver.\n";
static char sx__str1038[]=" ---\n\n ## Control Flow\n\n ```\n // if / elif / else\n if x > 0 {\n     // ...\n } elif x == 0 {\n     // ...\n } else {\n     // ...\n }\n\n // infinite loop with break\n for {\n     if done { break }\n }\n\n // for-in over a list\n for item in my_list {\n     // use item\n }\n\n // early return\n return value\n ```\n\n ---\n\n ## Pattern Matching\n\n ```\n // option\n match maybe_val {\n     some v => { /* v is the unwrapped value */ }\n     none   => { }\n }\n\n // result\n match result_val {\n     ok v  => { }\n     err e => { }\n }\n\n // union\n match shape {\n     Circle r    => { }\n     Rectangle w h => { }\n }\n ```\n";
static char sx__str1039[]=" ---\n\n ## Type Definitions\n\n ```\n // struct\n pub type Point = {\n     x: mut f64\n     y: mut f64\n }\n\n // union (tagged)\n pub union Shape = {\n     Circle(f64) | Rectangle(f64, f64)\n }\n\n // enum\n pub enum Color = {\n     Red\n     Green\n     Blue\n }\n ```\n\n ---\n\n ## Modules\n\n ```\n // import a module\n val io = use(\"std/stdio\")\n val mymod = use(\"path/to/module.sx\")\n\n // use it\n io.puts(\"hello\")\n\n // pub makes a declaration visible to importers\n pub fn my_fn() void = { }\n ```\n";
static char sx__str1040[]=" ---\n\n ## Contracts\n\n ```\n fn divide(a: i64, b: i64) i64 = {\n     pre  { nonzero: b != 0 }\n     post { result_valid: true }\n     return a / b\n }\n\n // guarded: contract violation checks persist in -release builds\n fn safe_div(a: i64, b: i64) i64 = {\n     guarded pre { nonzero: b != 0 }\n     return a / b\n }\n\n // type level invariant: checked at compile time where possible\n type SomeType = {\n     x: i32\n     y: i32\n\n     invariant {\n         x > 10 && y > 5\n     }\n }\n";
static char sx__str1041[]=" ---\n\n ## Builtins\n\n | Builtin                        | Description                              |\n |-------------------------------|------------------------------------------|\n | `@alloc(n)`                   | allocate n bytes, returns `ref void`, remember it is always better to use an allocator     |\n | `@realloc(ptr, n)`            | resize allocation                        |\n | `@free(ptr)`                  | free allocation                          |\n | `@sizeof(T)`                  | size of type T in bytes                  |\n | `@memcpy(dst, src, n)`        | copy n bytes                             |\n | `@memset(dst, val, n)`        | fill n bytes with val                    |\n | `@memmove(dst, src, n)`       | overlapping-safe copy                    |\n | `@ptradd(ptr, n)`             | pointer arithmetic                       |\n | `@load(ptr)`                  | load word from pointer                   |\n | `@store(ptr, val)`            | store word to pointer                    |\n | `@load8(ptr)`                 | load byte from pointer                   |\n | `@store8(ptr, val)`           | store byte to pointer                    |\n | `@bitcast(T, expr)`           | unsafe bitcast expr to type T            |\n | `@append(list, val)`          | append to list                           |\n | `@remove(list, i)`            | remove element at index i                |\n | `@get(list, i)`               | get element at index (returns option)    |\n | `@len(list)`                  | list length (returns usize)              |\n | `@reserve(list, n)`           | reserve capacity                         |\n | `@capacity(list)`             | current capacity                         |\n | `@fmtalloc(fmt, {args})`      | heap-allocate a formatted string         |\n | `@snprintf(buf, n, fmt, {})` | printf into a buffer                     |\n | `@print(fmt, {args})`        | print to stdout                          |\n | `@args()`                     | command-line arguments as `list[ref u8]` |\n";
static char sx__str1042[]=" ---\n\n ## Writing Idiomatic, Allocator-Aware Code\n\n Prefer passing an allocator into functions that create heap values\n instead of calling `@alloc` or `@fmtalloc` directly. The function\n should allocate from the allocator it is given and let the caller\n decide how long the memory lives. `spectrelib/fmt/core.sx` is a\n complete reference: its public formatting helpers are generic over\n `allocators.Allocator` and never free memory they do not own.\n\n ```\n val allocators = use(\"std/allocators\")\n val fmt        = use(\"std/fmt\")\n val string     = use(\"std/string\")\n\n pub fn greeting[A: allocators.Allocator](ac: A, name: ref u8) string.String = {\n     return fmt.format[A](ac, \"hello {s}\", {name})\n }\n\n pub fn main() void = trust {\n     val arena: mut allocators.Arena = allocators.Arena.new(4096 as usize)?\n     val msg = greeting[allocators.Arena](arena, \"world\")\n     @print(\"{s}\\n\", {string.cstr(msg)})\n     allocators.Arena.free(arena)\n }\n ```\n\n Rules of thumb:\n\n - Do not use `@fmtalloc` in a loop or in a short-lived helper unless\n   you also call `@free` on the result.\n - Use `allocators.Arena` for phase-scoped work: allocate freely, then\n   free the whole arena once. Use `allocators.Stack` when you need\n   nested checkpoints, and `allocators.HeapAllocator` when individual\n   allocations must be freed separately.\n - A function that returns owned memory should either take an allocator\n   or clearly document that the caller owns and frees the result.\n - Lists created with `@append` are not arena-backed; free their\n   backing storage separately when they are no longer needed.\n - Keep pointers valid for as long as they are used. Freeing an arena\n   invalidates every pointer allocated from it.\n";
static char sx__str1043[]=" ---\n\n ## The `trust` Keyword\n\n `trust expr` evaluates `expr` while suppressing all contract checks.\n Use it when you have external guarantees that contracts cannot express.\n\n ```\n val ptr: ref void = trust @load(some_raw_ptr)\n ```\n\n ---\n\n ## `assert`\n\n ```\n assert x > 0           // aborts if false\n assert my_fn() == ok 1\n ```\n\n ---\n\n ## Platform Conditionals\n\n ```\n when linux   {  }\n when darwin  {  }\n when windows {  }\n when posix   {  }\n ```\n\n ---\n\n ## Extern Functions\n\n ```\n extern (C) fn strlen(s: ref u8) i64 = \"strlen\"\n extern (C) fn printf(fmt: ref u8, ...) i32 = \"printf\"\n ```\n\n ---\n\n ## Tests\n\n ```\n test {\n     assert add(1, 2) == 3\n }\n ```\n\n Run with: `spectre <file.sx> -test`\n";
static char sx__str1044[]=" ---\n\n ## sx.mod — This is the Project Manifest\n\n ```\n entry \"./src/main.sx\"\n version \"v0.0.1\"\n spectre_version \"v0.0.12\"\n\n // Applied when this file is compiled/run directly.\n single_flags \"-dynamic -show-cmd\"\n\n dep \"https://github.com/user/some-lib\"\n\n build release {\n     flags  \"-release\"\n     output \"./myapp\"\n }\n\n build dev {\n     output \"./myapp-dev\"\n     env {\n         GIT_HASH=abcdef\n     }\n     posix {\n         action \"./scripts/notice.sh\"\n     }\n     windows.x86_64 {\n         action \"./scripts/notice.cmd\"\n     }\n }\n ```\n\n - `entry`   — source file passed to the compiler\n - `version` — project version string (available as `env.get(\"SPECTRE_MOD_VERSION\")`)\n - `spectre_version` — required Spectre compiler version\n - `single_flags` — default flags for standalone compile/run of files in this directory\n - `dep`     — git dependency URL (cloned to `lib/`)\n - `build`   — named build configuration\n   - `action`  — base shell command for this build config\n   - `flags`   — extra flags appended to the build command\n   - `output`  — `-o` output path appended to the build command\n   - `env`     — compile-time environment variables for this build\n   - `posix { ... }`, `windows { ... }` — per-platform overrides\n   - `posix.aarch64 { ... }`, `windows.x86_64 { ... }` — per-platform/arch overrides\n\n Platform selectors: windows, linux, darwin, freebsd, openbsd, netbsd,\n dragonflybsd, android, haiku, redox, plan9.\n Group selectors: posix/unix (linux, darwin, the BSDs, android, haiku, redox)\n and bsd (freebsd, openbsd, netbsd, dragonflybsd).\n\n Platform/arch blocks override individual fields from the outer build\n config. Matching blocks are applied in declaration order.\n";
